--- !u!114 &1482583835
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1482583833}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 6ba2ac043a0776a4d846aaf6ba38a734, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  title: Lathe machine
  description: 'This is where the rotating body of the wheel is cut. Because multiple
    tools (bits) are mounted on the tool turret, several machining processes can
    be performed continuously without manual tool changes.

    Roughing and Finishing
    the Wheel Surface:

    First, a hard bit roughly cuts the material to remove
    unnecessary parts (this is called roughing).

    Then, the machine automatically
    switches to a sharper bit to smooth the surface and create the final shape (this
    is called finishing).


    This lathe machine has spindle, 3 jaw and tool turret.



    The
    spindle is the main rotating shaft of a machine tool, providing the power for
    all machining operations. In a lathe, the spindle rotates the workpiece, such
    as a wheel. In a milling machine, it rotates the cutting tool, like an end mill.

    A
    jaw is part of a chuck that clamps and holds the workpiece firmly in place on
    the spindle. Jaws are adjustable and come in different types, such as 3-jaw or
    4-jaw, to fit various shapes and sizes of workpieces.


    A lathe tool, also
    known as a cutting tool or bit, is a single-point tool used to remove material
    from a spinning workpiece.

    Held stationary by the tool turret, it is pressed
    against the rapidly rotating workpiece. The tool''s cutting edge then shaves
    off material, shaping the workpiece into a desired form like a cylinder or a
    flat face.


    A tool turret is a rotating part of a lathe that holds multiple
    cutting tools. It can automatically and quickly switch between tools during a
    machining process.

    The main purpose of a tool turret is to improve efficiency
    and productivity. It eliminates the need for a person to manually change tools
    for each step, allowing for continuous, multi-stage operations like drilling,
    turning, and threading to be completed on a single workpiece.



    The robotic
    arm picks up a wheel from the first conveyor and places it in the lathe machine.

    A
    3-jaw chuck grips and holds the wheel, and the spindle begins to rotate it.

    The
    tool turret then moves in to process the wheel, with its cutting tools shaving
    material from the spinning wheel.

'
  type: 0
  url: 
